<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  WeatherKit, used for two things: the conditions strip on the Record tab,
	  and the observed wind offered for a saved session in place of the one
	  guessed from the shape of the track.

	  This file is one of three places WeatherKit has to be switched on, and the
	  other two are both at developer.apple.com → Certificates, Identifiers &
	  Profiles → Identifiers → com.laan.labs.openWater. That page has two tabs,
	  and WeatherKit appears on each of them under the same name:

	    Capabilities  — what gets written into the provisioning profile, so the
	                    signed binary is allowed to ask.
	    App Services  — what the token service checks when the app actually
	                    asks. This is the one that is easy to miss.

	  Enabling only the Capabilities tab produces a build that looks correct
	  from every angle you can inspect locally — the entitlement is in the
	  binary, the entitlement is in the profile — and still fails every single
	  call with:

	    WeatherDaemon.WDSJWTAuthenticatorServiceListener.Errors Code=2 "(null)"

	  which says nothing about which half is missing. It cost about three weeks
	  here, on the theory that a service-side registration was stuck, because
	  the App Services tab leaves no artifact on this machine to check against.
	  Regenerate the profile after changing either tab.

	  Without all three WeatherKit returns an error on every call. That is
	  handled — the strip hides itself and the session keeps its estimated wind —
	  but nothing about the app depends on it, which is the point.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.weatherkit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
